<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Confederate States of America</h1><p class="st-meta">1861 CE – 1865 CE · capital Richmond</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The breakaway republic of eleven slave-holding Southern states, whose defeat in the American Civil War ended slavery in the United States.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a>, who opposed the expansion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, won the 1860 election, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> seceded from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> in December. Six more Deep South states followed and formed the Confederacy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montgomery-alabama">Montgomery, Alabama</a>, in February 1861. After the attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fort-sumter">Fort Sumter</a> in April, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkansas">Arkansas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a> and North <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-carolina">Carolina</a> joined, and the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richmond-virginia">Richmond</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Confederate constitution copied much of the American one but explicitly protected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jefferson-davis">Jefferson Davis</a> governed a weak central state that nonetheless introduced conscription in 1862, taxes in kind and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paper-money">paper money</a> that led to severe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a>. The Union <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> throttled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> trade, and hopes of European recognition failed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-e-lee">Robert E. Lee's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-northern-virginia">Army of Northern Virginia</a> won major victories until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gettysburg">Gettysburg</a> in 1863.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>With the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-vicksburg">Vicksburg</a>, William T. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-tecumseh-sherman">Sherman</a>'s march through <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/georgia">Georgia</a> and the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richmond-virginia">Richmond</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-e-lee">Lee</a> surrendered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-appomattox-court-house">Appomattox</a> in April 1865 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jefferson-davis">Davis</a> was captured in May. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirteenth-amendment">Thirteenth Amendment</a> abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, and the Confederacy's memory remains bitterly contested.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/confederate-states-of-america.jpg" alt="Confederate States of America" width="320" height="178"><figcaption>Image: Original: Nicola Marschall (1829–1917) Vector: Ariane Schmidt · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Confederate_States_(1861%E2%80%931863).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1861 CE – 1865 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Richmond</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jefferson Davis, Alexander H. Stephens</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Confederacy, CSA</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1863&amp;focus=confederate-states-of-ameri